WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.445
Time boundaries define when attention is available, when it is protected, and when it is off limits.

2
00:00:06.445 --> 00:00:14.578
Boundaries can apply to meetings, messages, focus blocks, work hours, recovery, family commitments, and decision windows.

3
00:00:14.578 --> 00:00:16.650
A boundary is not a hostile wall.

4
00:00:16.650 --> 00:00:21.483
It is an agreement that helps people understand capacity and reliability.

5
00:00:21.483 --> 00:00:28.005
Without boundaries, availability can become a default that quietly consumes all other priorities.

6
00:00:28.005 --> 00:00:32.455
Calendar hygiene means keeping commitments visible and realistic.

7
00:00:32.455 --> 00:00:35.754
If focus time matters, it should appear somewhere.

8
00:00:35.754 --> 00:00:40.205
If a meeting needs preparation, that preparation should be counted.

9
00:00:40.205 --> 00:00:45.652
If a deadline requires review, the review should not be assumed to happen magically.

10
00:00:45.652 --> 00:00:48.414
A calendar is not just a record of meetings.

11
00:00:48.414 --> 00:00:53.018
It is an operating model for attention, commitments, and constraints.

12
00:00:53.018 --> 00:00:56.931
Meeting discipline protects the time of everyone involved.

13
00:00:56.931 --> 00:01:04.297
A useful meeting has a purpose, a scope, the right participants, and a decision or outcome that justifies the cost.

14
00:01:04.297 --> 00:01:11.893
A one-hour meeting with eight people is not one hour of time; it is eight working hours, plus preparation and recovery.

15
00:01:11.893 --> 00:01:15.192
Reducing unnecessary meetings is not antisocial.

16
00:01:15.192 --> 00:01:17.954
It is a basic form of professional respect.

17
00:01:17.954 --> 00:01:21.790
Commitments should be explicit, visible, and reviewable.

18
00:01:21.790 --> 00:01:31.535
Silent overcommitment often happens when people accept work without renegotiating scope, deadline, quality level, or existing responsibilities.

19
00:01:31.535 --> 00:01:36.675
Reliable people do not create reliability by pretending capacity is infinite.

20
00:01:36.675 --> 00:01:46.420
They create it by clarifying what is being promised, what tradeoffs are required, and when a commitment needs to be revised before it becomes a failure.
